      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the Pythagorean theorem, which states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nIn this problem, we know that one of the sides of the triangle is 6 cm and another side is 8 cm. To find the shortest possible length of the remaining side, we will assume that the side of length 8 cm is the hypotenuse.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we have:\n\n8^2 = 6^2 + x^2\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n64 = 36 + x^2\n\nSubtracting 36 from both sides, we have:\n\n28 = x^2\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\nx = √28\nx ≈ 5.29 cm\n\nTherefore, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is approximately 5.29 cm. \n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{5.29}",
