      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "For a right triangle, you can use the Pythagorean Theorem to relate the lengths of the sides. You know one of the sides (8 cm), so to get the length of the hypotenuse of the triangle, you can plug up to hypotenuse (h=cm) into this equation: \nh<sup>2</sup> = 6<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup> \nSolving, we get h <sup>2</sup> = 100, or h=10. Since the hypotenuse is longer than the sides of the triangle, this figure is an obtuse triangle. \nThe obtuse triangle is nonhomologous, or has 2 right angle pairs. Since the right angle is adjacent to the hypotenuse, this means the other is the base of the hypotenuse. The shortest side must be adjacent to the side of measure 8 cm. \nUsing the equation for right triangles from the Pythagorean Theorem, we have h<sup>2</sup> = 6<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup>. Once again, SOLVING, we get h<sup>2</sup> = 100, or h=10. Since this triangle forms an obtuse triangle, the hypotenuse is 10. The side adjacent to the hypotenuse must have the value of 8, so the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is 4.",
