      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we will apply the Pythagorean theorem. This theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nLet's denote the unknown side of the triangle as (x), the side with length 8 cm as (y), and the side with length 6 cm as (z), as shown in the figure.\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem:\nx^2 = y^2 + z^2\n\nInserting the given lengths of the sides:\nx^2 = 6^2 + 8^2\n\nSimplify the equation:\nx^2 = 36 + 64\n\nx^2 = 100\n\nSince x represents a length, it cannot be negative. Therefore, x = 10 if it's a distance.\n\nTherefore, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is 10.00 cm.",
