      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given that one side of the right triangle is 6 cm, another is 8 cm, and the right angle is opposite the unknown side (s), we can utilize the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that \\(a^2 + b^2 = c^2\\) in a right-angled triangle, where \\(c\\) is the hypotenuse and \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the legs.\n\nLet's define the unknown side as \\(s\\):\n\n\\[6^2 + s^2 = 8^2\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\n\\[36 + s^2 = 64\\]\n\nNow, subtract 36 from both sides:\n\n\\[s^2 = 64 - 36\\]\n\n\\[s^2 = 28\\]\n\nFinally, solving for \\(s\\):\n\n\\[s = \\sqrt{28} \\approx 5.29\\]\n\nSo, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is approximately 5.29 cm.",
